<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gondwana (India)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gondwana is the historical name of the hilly, forested region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> inhabited largely by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond people</a>, covering parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Between the 16th and 18th centuries Gond kingdoms such as Garha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandla">Mandla</a>, Deogarh and Chanda ruled much of it. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> absorbed most of the region, which later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gondwana-india.jpg" alt="Gondwana (India)" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Joppen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India_in_1525_Joppen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
